\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Scheme of the \texttt  {BRIDGE} coupling strategy. In the kick steps, \texttt  {FASTKICK} computes the mutual gravitational forces between stars and gas, and the velocities of both components are updated. In the drift step, each code evolves its particles independently: \texttt  {Ph4} handles stellar dynamics (including collision detection and mergers), while \texttt  {Fi} evolves the gas. After the kick-drift-kick cycle, the global \texttt  {BRIDGE} time step is updated based on the gas free-fall time and the coupling timescales ($t_{\rm  ff}$, $\Delta t_{\rm  coup, gas}$, $\Delta t_{\rm  coup, stars}$). This updated timestep is used for both the next \texttt  {BRIDGE} step and the \texttt  {Fi} integration. Additionally, escaped N-body particles are identified and tagged in the post-processing phase.}}{27}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{img:Bridge_diagram}{{3.5}{27}{Scheme of the \texttt {BRIDGE} coupling strategy. In the kick steps, \texttt {FASTKICK} computes the mutual gravitational forces between stars and gas, and the velocities of both components are updated. In the drift step, each code evolves its particles independently: \texttt {Ph4} handles stellar dynamics (including collision detection and mergers), while \texttt {Fi} evolves the gas. After the kick-drift-kick cycle, the global \texttt {BRIDGE} time step is updated based on the gas free-fall time and the coupling timescales ($t_{\rm ff}$, $\Delta t_{\rm coup, gas}$, $\Delta t_{\rm coup, stars}$). This updated timestep is used for both the next \texttt {BRIDGE} step and the \texttt {Fi} integration. Additionally, escaped N-body particles are identified and tagged in the post-processing phase}{figure.caption.16}{}}
